\begin_body

\begin_layout Title
Chapter 2: Abstract choice and preference theory
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

An 
\series bold
economy
\series default
 is a mathematical object in which agents, resources, firms, etc.
 (i.e., the elements that constitute the society) are represented.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

We start by studying the representation of agents.
 There are essentially two ways in which an agent is represented in economic
 models: (i) by means of his/her preferences; (ii) by means of his/her choices.
 We independently introduce them.
 We also study the relation between these two approaches.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
2.1
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Preference theory
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Our primitive is a set of alternatives 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

.
 In the preference model, an agent is represented by his/her statements
 of preference about the different alternatives in 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

.
 Let us introduce the mathematical structure that allows us to formalize
 this concept.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
2.1.1
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Mathematical aside: product space and binary relations
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition*

\series bold
(12).
 
\series default
\emph on
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

 be two sets.
 The 
\series bold
cartesian product of 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset


\series default
, denoted 
\begin_inset Formula $A\times B$
\end_inset

, is the set of all lists of two elements in which the first element belongs
 to 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 and the second element belongs to 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

, i.e., 
\begin_inset Formula $A\times B\equiv\left\{ (a,b):a\in A,\ b\in B\right\} $
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
2.1.2
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Binary relations
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

A binary relation is a mathematical object that represents statements between
 pairs of elements of a set.
 For instance, consider a set of alternatives 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

.
 For each two alternatives, say 
\begin_inset Formula $a$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $b$
\end_inset

, we collect the information whether an agent, say 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

, finds 
\begin_inset Formula $a$
\end_inset

 at least as good as 
\begin_inset Formula $b$
\end_inset

.
 We can then define the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

at least as good
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 binary relation as the collection of the postive answers.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition*

\series bold
(13).
 
\series default
\emph on
A 
\series bold
binary relation
\series default
 on a set 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 is a subset of the cross product space 
\begin_inset Formula $A\times A$
\end_inset

.
 The 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

at least as good
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 binary relation on a set 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 is defined as follows: 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim\equiv\left\{ (a,b)\in A\times A:i\ \mbox{finds \ensuremath{a}\ at least as good as \ensuremath{b}}\right\} \subseteq A\times A$
\end_inset


\emph default
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

In general, this formality is avoided by using short notation that represents
 the relation that holds between elements.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Note*
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

 is a set, 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim\ \subseteq X\times X$
\end_inset

.
 
\begin_inset Formula $x\succsim y\iff(x,y)\in\succsim$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition*

\series bold
(14).
 
\series default
\emph on
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 be a binary relation on a set 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
\emph on
Strict binary relation associated with 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

, denoted 
\begin_inset Formula $\succ$
\end_inset

.

\series default
 
\emph default

\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ \{a,b\}\subseteq A:a\succ b\iff\left[a\succsim b\ \wedge\ \neg(b\succsim a)\right]$
\end_inset

.

\emph on
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
\emph on
Symmetric Binary Relation associated with 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

, denoted 
\begin_inset Formula $\sim$
\end_inset

.

\series default
 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ \{a,b\}\subseteq A:a\sim b\iff\left[a\succsim b\ \wedge\ b\succsim a\right]$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

We refer to the strict relation associated to the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

at least as good as
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 relation as 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

preference,
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 as in 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

agent 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 preferes 
\begin_inset Formula $a$
\end_inset

 to 
\begin_inset Formula $b$
\end_inset

;.
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 We refer to the symmetric relation associated to the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

at least as good as
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 relation as 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

indifference,
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 as in 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

agent 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 is indifferent between 
\begin_inset Formula $a$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $b$
\end_inset

.
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

We usually assume in economics that binary relations that represent agent's
 rankings of alternatives satisfy two consistency properties: completeness
 and transitivity.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition*

\series bold
(15).
 
\series default
\emph on
A binary relation 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 on 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

 is 
\series bold
complete
\series default
 iff 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ \{x,y\}\subseteq X:x\succsim y\ \vee\ y\succsim x$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The assumption that 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 is complete says that the individual has a well-defined preference between
 any two possible alternatives.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition*

\series bold
(16).
 
\series default
\emph on
A binary relation 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 on 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

 is 
\series bold
transitive
\series default
 iff 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ \{x,y,z\}\subseteq X:\left[x\succsim y\ \wedge\ y\succsim z\right]\implies x\succsim z$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Transitivity implies that it is impossible to face the decision maker with
 a sequence of pairwise choices in which her preferences appear to cycle.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition*

\series bold
(17).

\series default
 
\emph on
A 
\series bold
rational preference relation
\series default
 is a complete and transitive binary relation.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

In the next section, we will see that when the set on which agents have
 preferences is finite, one can think of a preference as a ranking of the
 alternatives.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
2.2
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Utility theory
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Is it possible to condense all the information contained in a preference
 relation in a 
\emph on
welfare index
\emph default
? The study of this question is known as utility theory.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
2.2.1
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Mathematical aside: functions
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

A function from a set 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 to a set 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

 is a mathematical object that attaches to each element in 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 a unique element in 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

.
 The domain of a function from 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 to 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

 is the set 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 and its co-domain 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

.
 A function from 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 to 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

 is generically denoted 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

.
 To make explicit the domain and co-domain of function 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

, we usually write 
\begin_inset Formula $f:A\rightarrow B$
\end_inset

.
 The element attached by 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

 to 
\begin_inset Formula $x\in A$
\end_inset

 is denoted 
\begin_inset Formula $f(x)$
\end_inset

; we call 
\begin_inset Formula $f(x)$
\end_inset

 the image of 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 under 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

, or the value of 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

 at 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

.
 We also say that 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

 maps 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 to 
\begin_inset Formula $f(x)$
\end_inset

.
 A statement completely defining a function shows its name, domain, co-domain,
 and the image of each element in its domain.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition*

\series bold
(18).

\series default
 
\emph on
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $C$
\end_inset

 be three sets.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $g:A\rightarrow B$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $f:B\rightarrow C$
\end_inset

 be two functions.
 The 
\series bold
composite function of 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

and 
\begin_inset Formula $g$
\end_inset


\series default
, denoted 
\begin_inset Formula $f\circ g$
\end_inset

, is the function
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\begin{array}{cccl}
f\circ g: & A & \rightarrow & C\\
 & x & \mapsto & f\circ g(x)\equiv f(g(x))
\end{array}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
2.2.2
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Representation of preferences
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition*

\series bold
(19).
 
\series default
\emph on
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 be a binary relation on 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

.
 A function 
\begin_inset Formula $u:X\rightarrow\mathbb{R}$
\end_inset

 
\series bold
represents
\series default
 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 iff 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ \{x,y\}\subseteq X:x\succsim y\iff u(x)\geq u(y)$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Proposition*

\series bold
\emph on
(1).

\series default
\emph default
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 be a binary relation on 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

.
 If there is a function 
\begin_inset Formula $u:X\rightarrow\mathbb{R}$
\end_inset

 that represents 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

, then 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 is complete and transitive, i.e., it is a preference relation.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 be a binary relation on 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

.
 Assume 
\begin_inset Formula $\exists$
\end_inset

 a function 
\begin_inset Formula $u:X\rightarrow\mathbb{R}$
\end_inset

 that represents 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

.

\series bold
 
\series default
We want to show: 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ \{x,y\}\subseteq X:(x\succsim y)\vee(y\succsim x)$
\end_inset

, i.e., 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 is complete; 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ \{x,y,z\}\subseteq X:(x\succsim y)\wedge(y\succsim z)\implies(x\succsim z)$
\end_inset

, i.e., 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 is transitive.
\end_layout

