\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The ability to represent preferences by a utility function is closely linked
 to the assumption of rationality.
 Utility functions induce rational preference relations.
 However, in general, not all rational preference relations can be described
 by some utility function.
 Only under certain conditions to the alternative space do rational preferences
 imply the existence of a utility function representation.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

A property of a real-valued function is said to be ordinal if it is preserved
 under strictly increasing transformations.
 Cardinal properties are those not preserved under all such transformations.
 The following proposition states that the preference relation associated
 with a real-valued function is an ordinal property of the function.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proposition*

\series bold
\emph on
(2).

\series default
\emph default
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 be a binary relation on 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

 that is represented by a function 
\begin_inset Formula $u:X\rightarrow\mathbb{R}$
\end_inset

.
 For each strictly increasing function 
\begin_inset Formula $f:\mathbb{R}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}$
\end_inset

, we have that 
\begin_inset Formula $f\circ u$
\end_inset

 represents 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 be a binary relation on 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

 that is represented by a function 
\begin_inset Formula $u:X\rightarrow\mathbb{R}$
\end_inset

.
 By Proposition 1, 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 is complete and transitive.
 We want to show that 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ f:\mathbb{R}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}$
\end_inset

 such that 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

 is increasing, 
\begin_inset Formula $f\circ u$
\end_inset

 represents 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

.
 Take an increasing function 
\begin_inset Formula $f:\mathbb{R}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}$
\end_inset

.
 Take 
\begin_inset Formula $\{x,y\}\subseteq X$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula $(\Rightarrow)$
\end_inset

 Assume 
\begin_inset Formula $x\succsim y$
\end_inset

.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $u$
\end_inset

 represents 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $x\succsim y\iff u(x)\ge u(y)$
\end_inset

.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

 is an increasing function, then 
\family roman
\series medium
\shape up
\size normal
\emph off
\bar no
\strikeout off
\uuline off
\uwave off
\noun off
\color none

\begin_inset Formula $f(u(x))\ge f(u(y))$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula $(\Leftarrow)$
\end_inset

 Assume 
\begin_inset Formula $f(u(x))\ge f(u(y))$
\end_inset

.
 Suppose for contradiction 
\begin_inset Formula $\neg(x\succsim y)$
\end_inset

.
 Then, by the completeness of 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $y\succsim x$
\end_inset

.
 Now, by the definition of 
\begin_inset Formula $\succ$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\neg(x\succsim y)\wedge y\succsim x\iff y\succ x$
\end_inset

.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $u$
\end_inset

 represents 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $y\succ x\iff u(y)>u(x)$
\end_inset

.
 Then, 
\family roman
\series medium
\shape up
\size normal
\emph off
\bar no
\strikeout off
\uuline off
\uwave off
\noun off
\color none

\begin_inset Formula $f(u(y))>f(u(x))$
\end_inset

 since 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

 is an increasing function.

\family default
\series default
\shape default
\size default
\emph default
\bar default
\strikeout default
\uuline default
\uwave default
\noun default
\color inherit
 However, this is a contradiction since we assumed 
\begin_inset Formula $f(u(x))\ge f(u(y))$
\end_inset

 
\begin_inset Formula $(\rightarrow\leftarrow)$
\end_inset

.
 Thus, 
\begin_inset Formula $x\succsim y$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Therefore, for each increasing function 
\begin_inset Formula $f:\mathbb{R}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}$
\end_inset

, we have that 
\begin_inset Formula $f\circ u$
\end_inset

 represents 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Thus, the preference relation associated with a utility function is an ordinal
 property.
 On the other hand, the numeral values assocaited with the alternatives
 in 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

, and hence the magnitude of any differences in the utility measure between
 alternatives, are cardinal properties.
 Note that a utility funciton that represents a preference relation 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 is not unique.
 For any strictly increasing function 
\begin_inset Formula $f:\mathbb{R}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $v(x)=f(u(x))$
\end_inset

 is a new utility function representing the same preferences on 
\begin_inset Formula $u(\cdot)$
\end_inset

.
 It is only the ranking of alternatives that matters.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Note*
The existence of a utility functions imply rational preferences.
 But, in general, rational preferences do not imply the existence of a utility
 function.
 Though, under certain conditions, rational preferences do imply the existence
 of a utility function.
\end_layout

