A situation in which monotonicty may be violated is if an agent is a vegan
 and he prefers a vegetarian menu with less items over a strictly meat based
 menu that has several more options.
 A preference on 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

 can violate monotonicity.
 For example, we can define a preference relation as: 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim\equiv\{(A,B)\in X^{2}:|A|\leq|B|\}$
\end_inset

, i.e., 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ \{A,B\}\subseteq X:A\succsim B\iff|A|\leq|B|$
\end_inset

.
 Thus, 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ \{A,B\}\subseteq A:A\subseteq B\implies|A|\leq|B|\iff A\succsim B$
\end_inset

 .
 In this case, an agent finds a set with less elements at least as good
